  "title": "US Daily: Little Sign of Labor Market Mismatch So Far",
  "type": "economics",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-05-14",
  "insights": [
   "The report introduces a new occupation-level job openings measure to assess if AI adoption is creating a labor market mismatch, finding that mismatch has actually declined from its 2022 peak.",
   "US job openings have shifted since 2019: there are 300k more openings for on-site service/production roles and 450k fewer for office-based roles (sales, info services, admin) where AI is being deployed.",
   "Occupations highly exposed to AI substitution have seen openings fall sharply below pre-pandemic levels, while those with high augmentation potential or low exposure have seen more gradual declines.",
   "The decline in labor market mismatch is partly attributed to the fact that many AI-exposed occupations (where openings fell) started with large labor shortages, effectively normalizing the market.",
   "The report estimates the current labor market mismatch index would be 0.02 points higher without the impact of AI-driven shifts in job openings.",
   "While the first stage of AI deployment was fortuitously timed with labor shortages, the next stage will likely require greater workforce adaptation."
  ],
